---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const W = "worker/crates/iki-wasm";
const H = {
  short: "One Rust crate, the same bytes in the browser and the server",
  how: "The modules, where each runs, and its digest",
  rules: "Loading, building and checking",
  ref: "Files and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];
const modules = [
  {
    name: "iki_wasm.wasm",
    src: W,
    build: "scripts/build-wasm.sh",
    runs: "The browser's Web Worker; the server and the validator, under wasmtime",
    digest: "server/iki_wasm.wasm.sha256",
    adr: [90, 127],
  },
  {
    name: "plasmid-tools.wasm",
    src: `${W}/standalone/plasmid-tools`,
    build: "scripts/build-plasmid-tools-wasm.sh",
    runs: "iki/plasmid's actions: primers, melting temperatures, digests",
    digest: "plugins/plasmid/data/plasmid-tools.wasm.sha256",
    adr: [200],
  },
  {
    name: "dna-annotation.wasm",
    src: `${W}/standalone/dna-annotation`,
    build: "scripts/build-dna-annotation-wasm.sh",
    runs: "iki/plasmid's annotation action",
    digest: "plugins/plasmid/data/dna-annotation.wasm.sha256",
    adr: [206],
  },
  {
    name: "oligo-checks.wasm",
    src: `${W}/standalone/oligo-checks`,
    build: "scripts/build-oligo-checks-wasm.sh",
    runs: "iki/dna's primer checks",
    digest: "plugins/dna/data/oligo-checks.wasm.sha256",
    adr: [201],
  },
  {
    name: "oligo-measurement.wasm",
    src: `${W}/standalone/oligo.rs`,
    build: "scripts/build-oligo-wasm.sh",
    runs: "iki/dna's fixed-condition measurement",
    digest: "plugins/dna/data/oligo-measurement.sha256",
    adr: [],
  },
];
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      <Path p={W} label="iki-wasm" /> is a Rust crate compiled to WebAssembly. It holds CEL for
      plugins' rules and guards and apps' expressions, and parsers such as FASTA. The browser runs
      it in a Web Worker; the server and the plugin validator run the same bytes under wasmtime, so
      a rule means the same wherever it's checked (<Adr n={[12, 127, 128]} />). A few first-party
      actions also call small standalone modules, built from the same crates, under wasmtime on a
      worker.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Plugins don't ship Wasm of their own: their CEL runs in iki-wasm.</li>
      <li>Git holds each module's source and SHA-256, never its bytes.</li>
      <li>The server image carries precompiled native code, checked against its digest.</li>
    </ul>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Module</th><th>Built from</th><th>Built by</th><th>Runs in</th><th>Digest in git</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            modules.map((m) => (
              <tr>
                <td><code>{m.name}</code>{m.adr.length > 0 && <> (<Adr n={m.adr} />)</>}</td>
                <td><Path p={m.src} label={m.src.replace(`${W}/`, "")} /></td>
                <td><Path p={m.build} label={m.build.replace("scripts/", "")} /></td>
                <td>{m.runs}</td>
                <td><Path p={m.digest} /></td>
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      iki-wasm's exports are listed once, in <Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/wasm.py" sym="EXPORTS" />:
      checking expressions, checking and evaluating rules, which origin states a guard rules
      out, HELM, DNA annotation and melting temperature. It's Rust so the same code can run natively
      when Iki has a desktop app (<Path p={`${W}/src/lib.rs`} />, <Adr n="0088" />). Annotation's feature library, <Path
        p="worker/crates/iki-wasm/data/dna_features.json"
      />, is compiled in.
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="In the browser" open>
        <p>
          The page hands a file's bytes to the parsers' Web Worker and gets JSON back; the worker
          has no page to reach. It's created through a Trusted Types policy that only allows the
          app's own worker scripts (<Path p="web/app/src/reading.ts" />). <code>@ikisci/science</code>{" "}
          ships the same module for its parsers (<Path p="web/packages/science/src/parsers.ts" />,{" "}
          <PageLink to="Packages and the SDK" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="In the server: wasmtime, with fuel">
        <p>
          Apps' expressions are checked when an app is saved; plugins' rules and guards when a plugin
          is validated, and at each save and state change. Each thread has its own instance, and
          each call a budget of 200 million instructions' fuel, so no expression can hold anything
          up however it's written (<Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/wasm.py" sym="FUEL" />).
        </p>
        <p>
          In the server image the module is precompiled: <code>/opt/iki/wasm</code> holds <code
            >iki_wasm.cwasm</code
          > and its manifest, built for the image's platform; both platforms' digests are pinned
          in git. It's loaded only if every directory and file
          on that path is root-owned and unwritable, and the manifest names this host's target, the
          installed wasmtime version, fuel metering, and the SHA-256 of both the Wasm it was built
          from and the native code itself. Otherwise the server logs why and compiles the packaged
          Wasm instead (<Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/wasm.py" sym="def _image_module(" />, <Path
            p="scripts/build-server-aot.sh"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Standalone modules for plugins' actions">
        <p>
          An action's script loads its module from the plugin's <code>data/</code> directory and
          calls it under wasmtime (<Path p="plugins/dna/scripts/oligos.py" sym="MODULE" />). <code
            >git archive</code
          > of a pinned plugin has the digest but not the module, so <code>iki deploy build</code>{" "}
          takes the module from <code>iki_spec</code>, checks it against the digest in the plugin's
          tree, and writes it into the plugin before bundling it for workers. A module that is
          missing, has no digest or doesn't match is refused, so a worker gets bytes someone
          vouched for and needs no Rust (<Path p="cli/src/iki_cli/workers.py" sym="def prepared_source(" />,{" "}
          <Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/modules/__init__.py" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="SHA-256 in git, never the bytes">
        <p>
          <code>*.wasm</code> is ignored by git (<Path p=".gitignore" />), and stays that way: what's
          committed is the Rust, the build script and the digest. The digests are of modules built
          on Linux x86-64, where the builds are reproducible; on other hosts the scripts build for
          tests and say the digest isn't compared there (<Path
            p="scripts/build-plasmid-tools-wasm.sh"
          />).
        </p>
        <p>
          The server image builds every module in its <code>wasm</code> stage on linux/amd64, and its{" "}
          <code>verified-wasm</code> stage checks all of them against the digests before anything is
          copied on (<Path p="server/Dockerfile" />, <Path p="scripts/verify-server-wasm.sh" />). CI
          builds that payload once, in a reusable workflow, verifies it, and gives the same files to
          both platforms' image builds (<Path p=".github/workflows/build-server-wasm.yml" />); the
          Rust job rebuilds each standalone module with <code>--check</code> (<Path
            p=".github/workflows/ci.yml"
          />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Building it locally">
        <p>
          <Path p="scripts/build-wasm.sh" /> builds iki-wasm for <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code>{" "}
          with rustup and the toolchain <Path p="worker/rust-toolchain.toml" /> names, copies it to
          the web app, <code>@ikisci/science</code> and <code>iki_spec</code>, and builds the oligo
          measurement. Each standalone module has its own script.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <div>
        <h3>Files</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Path p={W} /> the crate, and <Path p={`${W}/standalone`} label="standalone/" /> modules</li>
          <li><Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/wasm.py" /> the server's and validator's loader</li>
          <li><Path p="spec/src/iki_spec/modules" /> where built standalone modules go</li>
          <li><Path p="server/iki_wasm.amd64.cwasm.sha256" />, <Path p="server/iki_wasm.arm64.cwasm.sha256" /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Decisions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><Adr n="0012" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0088" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0090" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0127" title /></li>
          <li><Adr n="0200" title /></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
